<body>

  {% include "nav.njk" %}

  <main>

    <!-- PAGE HERO -->
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="container reveal">
        <a class="back-link" href="/resources">&larr; Back to Resources</a>
        <span class="eyebrow">Mental Health Resources</span>
        <h1>What <em>Burnout</em> Actually Is</h1>
        <p class="lead">Not tiredness, and not a character flaw. A clear look at what sustained depletion does to a person, and why the usual advice stops working.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHAT IT IS -->
    <section class="section" id="what-is-burnout">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="reveal" style="max-width: 860px;">
          <span class="eyebrow">What Is It</span>
          <h2>Depletion, not weakness.</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">Burnout is what happens when demand outlasts recovery for long enough that the system stops replenishing. It is not the result of a hard week. It develops over months or years of output that exceeds input, in work, in caregiving, in any role where stopping did not feel like an option.</p>
          <p class="section-lead">The people who reach burnout are rarely the ones who did too little. They are usually the ones who were reliable, who absorbed more when things got difficult, and who kept going past the point where the body was asking them not to. That reliability is exactly what allows burnout to build unnoticed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal" style="margin-top: 52px;">
          <span class="myth-intro">Burnout is not:</span>
          <div class="myth-busted">
            <span class="myth-tag">Ordinary tiredness</span>
            <span class="myth-tag">Poor time management</span>
            <span class="myth-tag">A sign you cannot handle it</span>
            <span class="myth-tag">Fixed by one good weekend</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal">
          <div class="stat-panel">
            <div class="stat-cell">
              <span class="stat-label">How it builds</span>
              <strong>Gradual</strong>
              <span>Over months or years, rarely in a single hard week.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-cell">
              <span class="stat-label">What it is not</span>
              <strong>Not depression</strong>
              <span>Related and overlapping, but clinically distinct.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-cell">
              <span class="stat-label">The outlook</span>
              <strong>Recoverable</strong>
              <span>Though not by rest alone, and not by a long weekend.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- THE FULL PICTURE -->
    <section class="section dark-section" id="how-it-affects">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Full Picture</span>
        <h2>Burnout reaches further than work.</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Burnout starts in one domain and rarely stays there. What begins as depletion at work becomes shortness at home, then withdrawal from the people and activities that used to restore you, until the recovery sources themselves are gone.</p>

        <div class="hub" role="img" aria-label="Burnout at the center, connected to four areas it affects: the body, your thinking, what you do, and what you feel.">
          <div class="hub-core" aria-hidden="true"><span>Burnout</span>
            <svg class="hub-lines" viewBox="0 0 260 260" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <g stroke="rgba(216,184,102,.42)" stroke-width="1" stroke-linecap="round">
                <line x1="64.2" y1="92" x2="-13" y2="47.4"/>
                <line x1="195.8" y1="92" x2="273" y2="47.4"/>
                <line x1="64.2" y1="168" x2="-13" y2="212.6"/>
                <line x1="195.8" y1="168" x2="273" y2="212.6"/>
              </g>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="hub-node hub-n1">
            <span class="label">Body</span>
            <ul>
              <li>Tired past what sleep fixes</li>
              <li>Headaches, tension, illness</li>
              <li>Wired and exhausted at once</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="hub-node hub-n2">
            <span class="label">Thoughts</span>
            <ul>
              <li>Fog and lost detail</li>
              <li>What is the point of this</li>
              <li>Cynicism about the work</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="hub-node hub-n3">
            <span class="label">What You Do</span>
            <ul>
              <li>Going through the motions</li>
              <li>Withdrawing from people</li>
              <li>Dropping what restored you</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="hub-node hub-n4">
            <span class="label">Feelings</span>
            <ul>
              <li>Flat, rather than sad</li>
              <li>Short-tempered over nothing</li>
              <li>Detached from your own life</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="impact-grid">
          <div class="impact-card reveal">
            <span class="label">Body</span>
            <h3>Physical Signs</h3>
            <ul class="impact-list">
              <li>Exhaustion that sleep does not resolve</li>
              <li>Frequent headaches or muscle tension</li>
              <li>Getting sick more often</li>
              <li>Disrupted or unrefreshing sleep</li>
              <li>Appetite and digestion changes</li>
              <li>Tired and unable to settle at once</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="impact-card reveal">
            <span class="label">Thoughts</span>
            <h3>Cognitive Signs</h3>
            <ul class="impact-list">
              <li>Difficulty concentrating</li>
              <li>Forgetting routine details</li>
              <li>Decisions feeling disproportionately hard</li>
              <li>Cynicism about work that mattered</li>
              <li>A sense of pointlessness</li>
              <li>Harsh self-assessment about output</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="impact-card reveal">
            <span class="label">Behavior</span>
            <h3>Behavioral Signs</h3>
            <ul class="impact-list">
              <li>Doing the minimum to get through</li>
              <li>Withdrawing from colleagues and friends</li>
              <li>Dropping exercise, hobbies, rest</li>
              <li>Procrastinating on small tasks</li>
              <li>Working longer with less to show</li>
              <li>Using alcohol or scrolling to switch off</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="impact-card reveal">
            <span class="label">Feelings</span>
            <h3>Emotional Signs</h3>
            <ul class="impact-list">
              <li>Flatness rather than sadness</li>
              <li>Irritability out of proportion</li>
              <li>Detachment from people you care about</li>
              <li>Dread about the day ahead</li>
              <li>Guilt for not managing better</li>
              <li>Loss of satisfaction in good work</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- THE SHAPES IT TAKES -->
    <section class="section" id="types" style="background: var(--cream);">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">Where It Comes From</span>
        <h2>Burnout wears different uniforms.</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The mechanism is the same across contexts: sustained demand without adequate recovery. What differs is the setting, the reason stopping felt impossible, and the specific way it shows up.</p>
        <div class="light-cards-3" style="margin-top: 46px;">
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Occupational</small>
            <h3>Workplace Burnout</h3>
            <p>The most recognized form. Chronic workload, unclear expectations, or little control over how the work gets done. Effort stops correlating with outcome, and the job that once meant something starts feeling like something to survive.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Caregiver</small>
            <h3>Caregiver Burnout</h3>
            <p>Caring for a parent, a partner, or a child with ongoing needs. The role has no shift end and no obvious stopping point, and asking for help can feel like an admission. Guilt frequently sits on top of the exhaustion.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Responder</small>
            <h3>First Responder and Medical</h3>
            <p>Repeated exposure to other people's worst days, in cultures where continuing is the expectation. Burnout here often overlaps with trauma exposure, and the two need different responses even though they arrive together.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Achievement</small>
            <h3>High-Functioning Burnout</h3>
            <p>Performance stays intact while everything underneath it erodes. From the outside nothing looks wrong, which is precisely the difficulty. The competence keeps others reassured and keeps the person from being asked how they are.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Digital</small>
            <h3>Digital and Attention Burnout</h3>
            <p>Constant availability and fragmented attention keep the nervous system in low-grade activation all day. Scrolling looks like rest and does not function as it, so the hours meant for recovery do not deliver any.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Parental</small>
            <h3>Parental Burnout</h3>
            <p>Sustained caregiving load without sufficient support, often paired with the belief that finding it difficult reflects badly on you as a parent. That belief is usually what delays anyone reaching out.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PULL QUOTE -->
    <section class="section" id="in-practice" style="padding-bottom: 0;">
      <div class="container reveal">
        <div class="quote-card" style="text-align: center; padding: 60px 40px;">
          <p style="max-width: 44ch; margin: 0 auto;">"I am not sad. I am just empty. I do the things I am supposed to do and none of it reaches me anymore."</p>
          <small>A common way burnout gets described</small>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- THE CYCLE -->
    <section class="section" id="the-cycle">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">How It Works</span>
        <h2>How burnout builds.</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Burnout is progressive, and each stage is a reasonable response to the one before it. Recognizing which stage you are in matters, because what helps early is meaningfully different from what helps late.</p>
        <div class="cycle-steps">
          <div class="cycle-step reveal">
            <div class="step-number">1</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Demand rises and you meet it.</h3>
              <p>Something increases: workload, caregiving, responsibility. You absorb it, because you are capable and because the alternative appears to be letting someone down.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cycle-step reveal">
            <div class="step-number">2</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Recovery gets cut first.</h3>
              <p>The things that restored you go quietly: exercise, unhurried meals, seeing friends, sleep. They feel optional in a way that obligations do not, so they are what gets spent.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cycle-step reveal">
            <div class="step-number">3</div>
            <div>
              <h3>You push harder to compensate.</h3>
              <p>Output starts requiring more effort, so you supply more effort. Longer hours, tighter discipline, less margin. For a period this works, which is what makes it so difficult to stop.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cycle-step reveal">
            <div class="step-number">4</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Rest stops restoring.</h3>
              <p>You take the weekend, the holiday, the day off, and return no better. This is the point people usually describe as burnout, and it signals depletion deep enough that ordinary rest no longer reaches it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cycle-step reveal">
            <div class="step-number">5</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Cynicism sets in.</h3>
              <p>Detachment arrives as protection. Caring less hurts less. The work or the role that once had meaning becomes something to endure, and the distance extends to people as well.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cycle-step reveal">
            <div class="step-number">6</div>
            <div>
              <h3>It reaches your sense of self.</h3>
              <p>The exhaustion stops feeling like a phase and starts feeling like a description of who you are. Ineffective, unmotivated, no longer good at this. That conclusion is a symptom of burnout rather than an accurate account of you.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHAT HELPS -->
    <section class="section dark-section" id="what-helps">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">What Recovery Requires</span>
        <h2>Rest is necessary and rarely sufficient.</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Most burnout advice recommends rest, which is why so much of it disappoints. Rest addresses the depletion but not the conditions that produced it, and untouched conditions rebuild the depletion within weeks.</p>
        <div class="cards-3" style="margin-top: 48px;">
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">01</span>
            <h3>Name what it is.</h3>
            <p>Burnout, depression, and trauma exposure overlap and require different responses. Sorting out which you are dealing with, or which combination, is the first genuinely useful step and often needs another set of eyes.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">02</span>
            <h3>Change something structural.</h3>
            <p>Recovery that does not alter workload, boundaries, or support is temporary by design. The specific change varies. The requirement that something actually change does not.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">03</span>
            <h3>Rebuild recovery deliberately.</h3>
            <p>The restorative things went first and will not return by themselves. They have to be put back on purpose, in small amounts, before there is energy that makes them feel warranted.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">04</span>
            <h3>Separate rest from numbing.</h3>
            <p>Scrolling, drinking, and streaming pass the time without restoring the system. They are understandable and they are not recovery. Actual rest usually feels less appealing at the outset.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">05</span>
            <h3>Address the belief underneath.</h3>
            <p>Most people in burnout are carrying a rule about what they owe, what saying no would mean, or what happens if they stop. The load is real, and so is the rule that made stopping unthinkable.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">06</span>
            <h3>Get support before it is severe.</h3>
            <p>Burnout responds considerably better earlier than later, and the people most likely to wait are the ones others rely on. Reaching out at the tired stage is more effective than reaching out at the empty one.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="final-cta">
      <div class="container reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Empty Is Not a Permanent State</span>
        <h2>Burnout is not who you are. It is what happened to you.</h2>
        <p>Therapy is a place to work out what depleted you, what has to change, and how to rebuild in a way that holds up after the first good week.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-gold" href="/intake">Book a Free 15-Minute Call</a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

